      "context": "Facts about the current router (core/council/referee.py, measured 2026-10-08):\n- Routing uses keyword lists per persona in core/council/councils.json. Each keyword is weighted by 1 / (number of personas that list it), multi-word phrases get 1.5x. Tier rules: questions go to tier 0; broad verbs (redesign, decide, migrate, should we...) go to a council; new-capability scope words plus strong matches in two councils go to tier 3; otherwise the best-scoring persona handles it.\n- Tuning set (validation/fixtures/routing/cases.json, 50 requests): 50/50 correct after tuning; 28/50 (56%) before the change.\n- Held-out set (holdout.json, 20 requests written after tuning, never tuned against): 16/20 (80%). Misses: \"Speed up the slow product search query\" went to the database architect instead of performance; \"Store the session token in an httpOnly cookie\" went to the design systems architect (because of the word \"token\"); \"Decide whether to move from SQLite to Postgres\" matched no keywords; a tier 3 feature request was treated as tier 1.\n- Every plan now reports confidence (low when the top match rests on one keyword, nothing matched, or the runner-up is close) and its evidence. Both held-out specialist misses were flagged low. Agents can override with --council or --persona.\n- Planning runs in the CLI (node), the MCP server (python) and the website build; it must stay deterministic, local and dependency-free (Python standard library only), and needs no API key.\n- The alternative under discussion: let the calling agent (the model already running Claude Code or Copilot) classify the request, with keyword routing as a fallback, or add an embedding/LLM classifier inside the framework.",
